  # AR-repository-architecture: Repository architecture and high-level overview
  
  This is the structural map of the repository: what the major components are, how
  they fit together, and how a change flows from request to released artifact. It
  is the entry point for understanding the system; the behavioral contract for
  each part is in the functional spec (§FS-repository-functional-spec) and the
  per-component module docs cited below.
  
  ## 1. Components
  
  | Component | Role | Spec |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | `metadata/` | The shipped product: per-artifact `index.json` and per-version `reachability-metadata.json`, plus the master library list and schemas. | §FS-metadata |
  | `tests/` suite | The tests that justify each metadata entry, plus the harness build logic and support library. | §FS-tests |
  | `stats/` | The derived per-version metrics mirror and Forge run records that feed the coverage dashboard. | §forge/FS-forge-run-metrics |
  | Test harness (TCK) | The Gradle task surface that validates, compiles, and tests each coordinate on the JVM and `native-image`. | §AR-test-harness |
  | Build infrastructure | The two-layer Gradle build that wires the harness, the convention plugins, scaffolding, and packaging. | §AR-build-infrastructure |
  | Infrastructure E2E tests | `testInfra`/`testAllInfra`, which exercise the whole task surface to prove the infrastructure itself works. | §AR-test-harness |
  | CI | GitHub Actions workflows that gate PRs, sweep metadata on a schedule, track upstream versions, scan images, and publish coverage and releases. | §AR-repository-ci |
  | Forge | The automation subproject that turns labeled issues into review-ready PRs. | §forge/AR-forge-architecture |
  
  ## 2. How work flows through the system
  
  A change enters as an issue (a support request or a compatibility failure) and
  leaves as released metadata:
  
  1. **Authoring.** A contributor or Forge scaffolds a coordinate, writes tests
     that fail without metadata, and collects metadata through the harness
     authoring tasks (§AR-test-harness, §FS-tests).
  2. **Local verification.** The harness validates the index and metadata and runs
     the JVM and native-image lanes for that one coordinate
     (§FS-repository-functional-spec.8.2).
  3. **Review and CI.** A single-library PR is gated by the matching CI workflows,
     which re-run the same harness tasks as the authority and enforce licensing,
     security, and non-regression rules (§AR-repository-ci,
     §FS-repository-functional-spec.5).
  4. **Recording coverage.** Newly passing versions are recorded into `index.json`
     and mirrored into `stats/`; the coverage dashboard is regenerated and
     published (§forge/FS-forge-run-metrics, §AR-repository-ci).
  5. **Release.** On a weekly cadence the metadata directory is packaged and
     attached as a numbered GitHub Release; metadata-changing `master` pushes can
     also refresh the floating `SNAPSHOT` release, which native-build-tools
     resolves at build time
     (§FS-repository-functional-spec.4, §GOAL-fresh-metadata).
  
  Two scheduled loops keep this self-sustaining: the full metadata sweep guards
  against regressions before releases, and the upstream-version tracker discovers
  new versions, records the passing ones, and files failure issues that become the
  Forge repair queue (§FS-repository-functional-spec.9, §AR-repository-ci).
  
  ## 3. Implementation overview
  
  - **Gradle, two layers.** A root harness layer owns coordinate selection,
    validation, reporting, matrix generation, and packaging; per-coordinate
    sub-builds compile and run each library's tests in isolation. Adding a library
    needs no build-logic change — discovery is metadata-driven
    (§AR-build-infrastructure, §AR-test-harness).
  - **GitHub Actions, matrix-driven.** All test workflows derive their JDK/OS
    matrix from `ci.json`, the single source of truth, and run the same Gradle
    tasks a contributor runs locally (§AR-repository-ci).
  - **Schemas everywhere.** Metadata, index, stats, and the framework list each
    validate against a vendored JSON schema. Minor and patch schema bumps stay
    backward-compatible so older consumers keep working; a major bump represents a
    change native-build-tools must adopt, released in lockstep with the plugin
    (§FS-metadata, §FS-repository-functional-spec.4).
  - **Separation of automation.** Forge is an independently checked subproject in
    its own grund namespace; from the repository's side it is just another
    contributor bound by the same constraints and gates
    (§forge/AR-forge-architecture, §FS-repository-functional-spec.4).
[tool-exec] end id=call_jwuvT8MGWCldDOj0rCM0ZLWF|fc_005f4d61f783f2e5016ab098d7368087d2a1fd7cfad1a1ae93 name=read status=success
  # PRCPL-prefer-algorithmic: Everything that can be algorithmic should be algorithmic
  
  When a rule, check, decision, or workflow step can be expressed as a
  deterministic algorithm, implement it as one — a schema, a validator, a build
  task, a CI gate, a script — rather than leaving it to prompt instructions,
  agent judgment, or reviewer vigilance. Judgment, human or model, is reserved
  for what cannot be decided mechanically.
  §GOAL-protect-shipped-metadata
  
  An algorithmic rule runs the same way every time, costs nothing per run, fails
  loudly in CI instead of quietly in review, and cannot drift the way prompt
  wording and reviewer attention do. Every rule left to judgment is a rule that
  is enforced only sometimes.
  
  Applying the principle:
  
  - Pair mechanically checkable spec points with an enforcing gate instead of
    restating them in prompts or review checklists.
  - Prefer mechanically checkable formulations — numeric limits, enumerable
    patterns, exact paths — over qualitative language, so a rule stated for
    judgment today can be promoted into a gate tomorrow.
  - When an agent or reviewer makes the same decision repeatedly, promote that
    decision into deterministic code and leave the agent only the judgment that
    remains.
  - Prompt instructions and review skills are the fallback for what genuinely
    requires judgment, and even then each instruction cites the spec point it
    enforces, so every rule keeps one checkable home.
  
  # PRCPL-verify-inputs: Define inputs exactly and verify them before running
  
  Every component declares its inputs exactly — a schema, a property list, a
  path set, a capability list — and verifies them at its boundary before doing
  any work. A component that runs on unverified inputs converts its caller's bug
  into its own mysterious failure, and in a system with this many components —
  harness, CI, Forge control plane, drivers, agents, publication — the debugging
  then happens in the wrong component. Fail fast at the boundary with a message
  naming the violated input contract, so a defect surfaces in the component that
  introduced it.
  §GOAL-protect-shipped-metadata
  
  Applying the principle:
  
  - Give every persisted or exchanged artifact a schema and validate on read,
    not just on write; a reader that trusts its writer inherits the writer's
    bugs.
  - Validate required properties, paths, and environment capabilities before the
    first side effect, and reject with the exact missing or malformed input, not
    a downstream stack trace.
  - When a failure is diagnosed to a missing input check, add the check to the
    component that consumed the input — not a workaround in the component where
    the failure happened to surface.
[recv] {"type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_ZRKSKz3eG5yrVFqduYbJjPCY|fc_005f4d61f783f2e5016ab098d7365087d283ec244bbc8841fb", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "  owns the chunk boundary. The skip is decided exactly once: the composite\n  records it on the continuation marker's `explore` phase, and descriptor\n  creation reads that phase instead of regenerating a report. The marker also\n  records the created issue number, so retries of one publication ID reuse one\n  follow-up issue.\n\nFor `library-new-request` issues and `library-update-request` issues routed to\ndynamic-access coverage improvement, `forge_metadata.py` must invoke the\nmatching orchestration script with the issue number and configured class\nthreshold. An iterative-only workflow may use a dispatcher-refreshed report to\nreduce that value to the current remaining class count before it starts.\n\nPreparation is not the decision. Before it decides anything, `forge_metadata.py`\nmust prepare the same dynamic-access report input for every chunk-eligible\nissue, whichever workflow was selected: it materializes the coordinate's test\nproject — scaffolding a new library, or resolving the library-update target —\nand refreshes the report from it. A run resumed on a preserved tree keeps that\ntree and only refreshes the report. Deferring the chunk decision to a bulk phase\nmust not skip this preparation, so no workflow starts against a report that was\nnever built (§AR-dynamic-access-fallback-and-failure). The deferral is\nlogged with the prepared report's uncovered class count alongside the boundary,\nso the log records what was measured and not only what was configured.\n\nA workflow with a bulk phase must make the chunk decision after the bulk\niteration budget and its native-test gates. The bulk phase keeps its\ninitial report as the baseline and uses the last successful iteration's already\nrefreshed report as the final report; it must not run another report solely for\nthe chunk decision. Classes uncovered in the baseline and covered in the final\nreport are completed by bulk. The remaining set is the final report's uncovered\nclasses minus the exhaust report and continuation marker's processed set.\n\nFor an optimistic composite, `--chunk-class-count` is the total class\nboundary for one non-final run across both phases. If the final remainder is no\nlarger than the configured boundary, iterative exploration finishes that\nremainder. Otherwise, if bulk completed at least the boundary, the workflow\nreturns chunk-ready without starting iterative exploration. If bulk completed\nless, iterative exploration receives only the shortfall. Its completed,\nskipped, exhausted, and failed classes all count toward that shortfall. Thus,\nwith a boundary of `15`, a bulk pass that completes `10` classes is followed by\nat most `5` iterative terminal classes, while a bulk pass that completes `20`\nclasses stops before iterative exploration unless no more than `15` classes\nremain.\n\nA pure-bulk strategy has no iterative phase with which to fill a shortfall. It\nreturns chunk-ready after a productive run when more than the threshold remain;\nif bulk covers no new class, or the remainder is no larger than the threshold,\nthe run is final so Forge cannot publish an unbounded sequence of identical\nbulk chunks.\n\nChunked mode is automatic after the issue is marked with the\n`chunked-dynamic-access` label. The normal project status remains the run-state\nsignal: `Todo` means Forge may claim the next chunk, `In Progress` means a chunk\nis currently being generated or reviewed, and the final PR's `Fixes: #<issue>`\ntransition moves the issue to `Done`. Forge must not require an explicit\nresume-state CLI flag; the exhaust report\nlocation must be derived from the coordinate and loaded automatically by the\norchestration scripts, as specified by §AR-dynamic-access-exhaust-report. When\nthe issue is being resumed from a preserved failed-run continuation marker,\nForge may proceed without a coordinate-local exhaust report and use\n`explore.exhaustedClasses` from the marker as the processed-class set for the\nresumed run (§FS-forge-run-continuation.2).\n\nWhen a non-final chunk PR merges, Forge releases the linked issue for the next\nchunk by moving it to `Todo`, clearing its assignees, and removing the\n`human-intervention` and `resumable` labels when present. It retains the\n`chunked-dynamic-access` label so the next claim stays in chunked mode.\n\nChunk PRs use `Refs: #<issue>` until the final chunk. Only the final chunk PR\nmay use `Fixes: #<issue>` and move the issue to `Done`. Non-final chunk PRs\nmust commit enough exhaust-report state for the next run to skip classes already\ncompleted, skipped, exhausted, or failed in earlier chunks\n(§AR-chunked-dynamic-access-pr-linking).\n\nBefore the single verified push, every chunk also records its publication ID\nand unique publication branch in the exhaust report. The publisher repeats the\nID in a machine-readable PR-body trailer. A later chunk loads the merged report,\nresolves the preceding PR by the exact head branch, verifies the matching\npublication ID and merged state, and checks that the merge commit is an ancestor\nof its new base. Forge must not create a second post-publication commit merely\nto store a GitHub-assigned PR number.\n\n## FS-forge-workflow-spec-catalog: Workflow specifications\n\nThe whole workflow system contract is §AR-forge-workflow-system. Each supported\nqueue (§FS-forge-scope) is entered by a deterministic workflow driver\n(§AR-forge-drivers) that prepares one run and delegates to the workflow\nengine governed by a workflow spec. The driver scripts and the workflow specs\nthey run:\n\n| Queue | Driver script | Workflow spec |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `library-new-request` | `add_new_library_support.py` | new library support (§AR-forge-driver-queues.1), which runs dynamic-access generation plus native metadata tracing and verification |\n| `library-update-request` | `improve_library_coverage.py`, or the missing-version router | dynamic-access coverage improvement (§AR-forge-driver-queues.2), Java repair (§AR-java-fail-fix-workflow), or native-image run repair (§AR-forge-driver-queues.4) depending on the compatibility probe |\n| `fails-javac-compile` | `fix_javac_fail.py` | Java failure repair (§AR-java-fail-fix-workflow) |\n| `fails-java-run` | `fix_java_run_fail.py` | Java failure repair (§AR-java-fail-fix-workflow) |\n| `fails-native-image-run` | `fix_ni_run.py` | native-image run repair (§AR-forge-driver-queues.4) |\n| code coverage improvement (planned) | — | code coverage improvement (§AR-code-coverage-improvement) |\n\nEach engine is bound to a named configuration bundle defined by\n§FS-forge-predefined-strategy-contract. The `basic_iterative` engine is not a\nseparate queue: it is the most basic workflow and the fallback the dynamic-access\nworkflow delegates to when a library turns out to have no dynamic access\n(§AR-basic-iterative).\n\nForge benchmarking is a top-level benchmark contract because it compares\ngeneration strategies across multiple `library-new-request` targets\n(§FS-forge-generation-benchmarking) and records cost (in service of\n§GOAL-minimize-generation-cost), token, iteration, LOC, coverage (in service\nof §GOAL-maximize-library-coverage), dynamic-access, and metadata metrics.\n\nThe implementation roadmap orders the first known Forge spec gaps to close; see\n§ROADMAP-forge-implementation.\n"}], "isError": false, "timestamp": 1789958359307}}
